In this lesson, we'll look at how to pick the right 2D picture for a 3D object, because each one is good at answering a different question. First up, the decision guide. If you need exact lengths, go with views, since they show true measurements with right angles. If you just want to picture what the object actually looks like, grab a perspective or isometric drawing, because that one shows depth. And if you're working out surface area, or planning to cut the shape out of paper, use a net — it lays every face out at full size. Next, why one representation isn't enough. A single 2D drawing can be sneaky. Different 3D objects can share the same view. For example, the front view of a cube and the front view of a tall square prism both look like rectangles — only the side view tells them apart. One quick warning: perspective drawings look real, but they don't give true measurements. Slanted edges are drawn shorter than they really are, so if you need exact sizes, switch to views. Now let's see it in action.